      "gap": "Durrett (author version 5, 11 January 2019) states with the definition of sigma-finiteness on an algebra (p. 4) that, putting A'1 = A1 and for n >= 2 either A'n = A1 union ... union An or A'n = An intersect the complements of A1, ..., An-1, the covering sets may be taken without loss of generality increasing to Omega or pairwise disjoint; and he gives as the important example of a semialgebra (p. 3, Example 1.1.5) S_d, the empty set together with all rectangles (a1, b1] x ... x (ad, bd] in R^d with -infinity <= ai < bi <= infinity. def:p15-semialgebra-premeasure states neither this replacement of the sigma-finite cover by an increasing or a disjoint one (the increasing form appears only inside the uniqueness proof annex:p15.premeasure-extension) nor the d-dimensional semialgebra example; the route has only d = 1 (def:p15-stieltjes-intervals) and finite-endpoint rectangles as a pi-system.",
